WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:08.970
In this video, I'm very pleased to introduce you to

2
00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.965
Dr. Richard Felder and Dr. Rebecca Brent,

3
00:00:11.965 --> 00:00:17.490
two educators who have had an enormous impact on the landscape of education.

4
00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:22.140
I first met Rich and Rebecca at the very beginning of my teaching career.

5
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.910
I was lucky enough to attend a workshop they taught on student-centered learning,

6
00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:30.660
that workshop changed the whole focus of my teaching and allowed me to

7
00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.460
understand learning in a whole new deeper way.

8
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.770
I think you'll find that your own understanding of

9
00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.150
learning will change as you listen to this interview.

10
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.435
Rich Felder has written the best selling book on

11
00:00:43.435 --> 00:00:48.020
Introductory Chemical Engineering in the country and possibly in the world.

12
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:50.870
He's also won the Lifetime Achievement Award from

13
00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:56.760
the American Society of Engineering Education for his enormously influential work,

14
00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.270
but don't make the mistake of thinking that Dr.

15
00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.200
Felder's work is only useful for engineers.

16
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.155
A big reason he's so influential is that the approaches

17
00:01:06.155 --> 00:01:10.545
he uses are applicable to virtually any field of study.

18
00:01:10.545 --> 00:01:15.570
It is actually Dr. Felder's enormously gifted wife, Dr. Rebecca Brent,

19
00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:20.640
the President of the education consulting firm, Education Designs,

20
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:27.005
and longtime director of the NSF Sponsored Succeed Coalition Faculty Development Program,

21
00:01:27.005 --> 00:01:28.300
who has helped spearhead

22
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:33.925
the broad multidisciplinary impact of the couples work on education.

23
00:01:33.925 --> 00:01:37.465
I think you'll find Dr. Felder and Dr. Brent's work to have

24
00:01:37.465 --> 00:01:41.980
as powerful an influence on you as it's had on me.

25
00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:46.900
So, you've written some extraordinarily helpful advice

26
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:50.345
for students about how to prepare for tests.

27
00:01:50.345 --> 00:01:54.840
If you could sum that advice up in a nutshell, what would you say?

28
00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.275
Let me first say something about how not to prepare for tests,

29
00:01:59.275 --> 00:02:07.260
which is probably what 90 plus percent of my students do to prepare for tests.

30
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:15.925
One, generally they ignore the book but they may read the book like a novel,

31
00:02:15.925 --> 00:02:19.970
just going flipping through it, maybe they underline,

32
00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:25.760
which as far as I can tell is a singularly useless strategy for doing anything.

33
00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.100
Then when they've done that,

34
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.945
maybe they look through the old homework solutions and read through them,

35
00:02:32.945 --> 00:02:34.390
again like a novel,

36
00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:36.199
and then imagine that they've studied,

37
00:02:36.199 --> 00:02:40.500
and that provides them with no help at all in preparing for the test.

38
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.300
First of all, the test is not going to be just the homework again.

39
00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.265
It's going to be new problems that they have to solve.

40
00:02:47.265 --> 00:02:51.210
So, if that's how not to do it, how do you do it?

41
00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:56.290
The only way that I've ever found that works to prepare for tests is,

42
00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.230
and I'm talking about problem-solving tests,

43
00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:01.315
not the kind that I give in engineering,

44
00:03:01.315 --> 00:03:03.040
is to work problems,

45
00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.845
to set problems up,

46
00:03:04.845 --> 00:03:06.615
not to work through them in detail.

47
00:03:06.615 --> 00:03:08.455
So, I tell the students,

48
00:03:08.455 --> 00:03:13.590
you can start with the homework problems that you solve and then solve it again.

49
00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.300
Set up the solution without looking back at

50
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:20.625
the worked-out solution that was graded and see how far you get.

51
00:03:20.625 --> 00:03:21.730
If you can set it up,

52
00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:25.910
so the only thing that's left to do is algebra or simple math,

53
00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.090
then you're done, then go on to another problem.

54
00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:30.670
Don't waste your time doing algebra and arithmetic,

55
00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:32.520
you're not learning anything from that.

56
00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.830
Then maybe go to your book and pick out another couple of

57
00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:39.000
problems and try to set those up.

58
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.810
The more of that you do,

59
00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.680
the better you're going to be at solving problems,

60
00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:50.460
the more likely you are to be able to solve the problems that show up on the test.

61
00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:56.095
The other thing that I strongly advise is work with other people.

62
00:03:56.095 --> 00:03:58.105
Get into a study group,

63
00:03:58.105 --> 00:04:00.180
make it a serious study group,

64
00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.570
and try to make sure that at least somebody in the study group is smarter than you

65
00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:10.120
are and really knows how to solve the problem so that if you get stuck on something,

66
00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.100
this person is likely to be able to figure it

67
00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.905
out and all of you go marching along together.

68
00:04:14.905 --> 00:04:18.095
Leave the beer and the snacks in the refrigerator.

69
00:04:18.095 --> 00:04:20.530
Just spend your time working through problems,

70
00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:22.630
setting them up, making sure you know how to solve it,

71
00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:24.760
going on doing more problems.

72
00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:29.440
Then, when you're either exhausted or you've covered as much as you need to cover,

73
00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:32.305
you can then hit the refrigerator.

74
00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:39.090
This is so much like what we've talked about throughout this course as far as simply,

75
00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:44.710
reading a book is not putting it necessarily in your brain,

76
00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:49.320
and to have to actively grasp the material,

77
00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.360
and working with others sometimes is

78
00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:55.310
a very good way to help reinforce what you're learning,

79
00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:58.895
and also filling gaps in what you're trying to learn about.

80
00:04:58.895 --> 00:05:00.655
So, I think that's

81
00:05:00.655 --> 00:05:04.845
a fabulous advice and I have to say thank you for that wonderful checklist.

82
00:05:04.845 --> 00:05:07.235
You're welcome.

83
00:05:07.235 --> 00:05:11.350
I think there's also some things you can do when you

84
00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:15.699
get in the test because sometimes it's a high pressure situation,

85
00:05:15.699 --> 00:05:18.325
you may feel a lot of anxiety.

86
00:05:18.325 --> 00:05:22.150
So, taking a moment when you get the test,

87
00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:24.435
looking through the whole thing,

88
00:05:24.435 --> 00:05:27.430
just to get a sense of where you're going.

89
00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:31.320
Pick out the easy questions and start there.

90
00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.275
Then if you get stuck somewhere,

91
00:05:34.275 --> 00:05:39.410
go on to something else that you feel confident you can handle.

92
00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:42.100
Keep moving, don't stop.

93
00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:47.770
Sometimes when we're anxious, we just freeze.

94
00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:52.375
Keep moving, keep working even if you can't get everything,

95
00:05:52.375 --> 00:05:53.740
though for partial credit.

96
00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:54.285
Right.

97
00:05:54.285 --> 00:06:00.095
Get some of it, put something down and that'll help you be more successful in the test.

98
00:06:00.095 --> 00:06:03.200
Also, I've had students who really felt

99
00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:08.305
rising anxiety if they got to a question that they couldn't answer right away.

100
00:06:08.305 --> 00:06:13.085
If you find yourself really getting upset, just stop,

101
00:06:13.085 --> 00:06:14.460
take a deep breath,

102
00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.010
pause for a moment,

103
00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:17.930
and then look back at it.

104
00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:21.250
Just calming down a little bit can go a long way.

105
00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:25.460
Oh, it can. Well, that's one of the things we've also talked about in the course,

106
00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:33.850
is breathing from the belly to help draw in and really do deep breathing,

107
00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:40.735
that's a very effective technique to neurophysiologically allow yourself to calm down.

108
00:06:40.735 --> 00:06:44.090
Another interesting thing, and I think for our viewers,

109
00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:52.055
this shows a bit of the difference in approach of different top quality educators.

110
00:06:52.055 --> 00:06:55.390
One thing we've talked about is, just touch,

111
00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:58.670
at the very beginning when you're starting to look through a test,

112
00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:04.560
touch on and start working some of the more difficult problems just until you get stuck.

113
00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:06.515
So, it takes willpower,

114
00:07:06.515 --> 00:07:08.500
you have to pull yourself off quickly as

115
00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:10.890
soon as you can detect that you're getting stuck.

116
00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:15.650
But that actually gets your focused attention on it,

117
00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:21.180
then pulls that attention away and when you're working another easier problem,

118
00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:24.100
it allows you to in the background,

119
00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:26.800
your diffuse mode is working away.

120
00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.040
Then when you revisit that more difficult problem,

121
00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:32.560
you've got a little bit more going

122
00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.420
on intellectually because you've had time to think about.

123
00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:39.970
If you wait for the hardest problems later in the tests,

124
00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:42.090
sometimes that can create problems.

125
00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:47.675
So, there's different approaches and I think different folks,

126
00:07:47.675 --> 00:07:51.230
if they're aware of the different kinds of approaches,

127
00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:56.085
it gives them more tools that they can use for their intellectual toolkits.

128
00:07:56.085 --> 00:08:01.880
Exactly. I think that's always important to look at all the advice,

129
00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:04.640
look at all the suggestions, try things,

130
00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:07.940
give them enough of the try that you see how well they work.

131
00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:11.780
S, I think that's important for your students.

132
00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:14.025
Always be doing.

133
00:08:14.025 --> 00:08:15.900
I would extend that actually.

134
00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:17.615
The advice you just gave,

135
00:08:17.615 --> 00:08:22.125
I think is life advice not just for taking tests.

136
00:08:22.125 --> 00:08:26.290
There's this magical phenomenon that I've experienced many times in my life.

137
00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:28.900
If I'm working on something,

138
00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:33.620
a problem when I was a student that might be a problem on a homework assignment,

139
00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:35.880
it could have been a problem on a test,

140
00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:39.920
it could be a problem in my doctoral research,

141
00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:42.110
or something I'm working on.

142
00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:48.320
When I went into research as a faculty member but I'm struggling with something,

143
00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.480
I can't make any headway on it,

144
00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.319
my natural tendency is just to keep grinding on it,

145
00:08:54.319 --> 00:08:57.455
keep banging my head against that concrete wall

146
00:08:57.455 --> 00:09:01.275
and it doesn't the damage to the wall at all.

147
00:09:01.275 --> 00:09:03.305
It's not too good for my head.

148
00:09:03.305 --> 00:09:09.860
What I found is when at of desperation ,or just because I needed a break or whatever,

149
00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:13.815
I stopped grinding on it and I went off and I did something else.

150
00:09:13.815 --> 00:09:16.200
I took a walk, I took a nap,

151
00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:18.770
I went to work on something else I was working on.

152
00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:20.625
Then when I came around later,

153
00:09:20.625 --> 00:09:22.580
got back to work on the problem,

154
00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:25.490
the solution was magically there.

155
00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:29.780
I tend to believe what I've read in cognitive science

156
00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:36.220
about all of the stuff going on underneath the conscious part that I'm aware of.

157
00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:40.010
Somewhere in that part of my brain is somebody or

158
00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:44.520
something that's a lot smarter than whatever I think of as me.

159
00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:46.985
Once I've gotten started on the problem,

160
00:09:46.985 --> 00:09:48.860
exactly as you've just described,

161
00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:50.155
and then I get away from it,

162
00:09:50.155 --> 00:09:54.495
and it's like I'm turning it over to that part of my brain which is smarter,

163
00:09:54.495 --> 00:09:57.145
and it somehow solves the problem.

164
00:09:57.145 --> 00:10:00.120
When I come back, there's the solution like magic.

165
00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:02.290
Yes, that's the idea.

166
00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:08.000
It's basically when you purposefully pull your attention off,

167
00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.459
or allow your attention to drift off,

168
00:10:10.459 --> 00:10:12.620
that actually opens access to

169
00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:18.350
the neural resting states that you need to be able to solve the problem.

170
00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:21.540
Just alluding to some of these same life issues,

171
00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:22.850
sometimes my husband and I,

172
00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:28.230
we'll be thinking about a problem that we have to solve, for example,

173
00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:31.480
how do we schedule interviews, how do we,

174
00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:36.305
and we'll say, you know we don't have to solve this tonight.

175
00:10:36.305 --> 00:10:39.750
Sure enough, within another day or two,

176
00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:41.480
the answers will come to us.

177
00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:46.250
MY mother you used to call there gave their subconscious an assignment.

178
00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:49.035
I often think about that.

179
00:10:49.035 --> 00:10:52.840
Your mother was very, very smart.